declare cid uuid;
begin
  select id into cid from public.courses where slug = 'bib-102';
  insert into public.course_items (course_id, kind, position, title, content) select cid, 'topic', 22, $j$6.2 The Throne in Heaven and the Lamb Who Was Slain$j$, $j${"readings": [{"title": "Revelation 4 (King James Version)", "url": "/portal/bible/Revelation/4"}, {"title": "Revelation 5 (King James Version)", "url": "/portal/bible/Revelation/5"}, {"title": "Revelation 7 (King James Version)", "url": "/portal/bible/Revelation/7"}], "body": "Read Revelation 4, Revelation 5 and Revelation 7 in the King James Version. Then read this lesson.\n\nIn chapters 4 and 5 John was taken in the Spirit to the throne room of heaven. He saw God on His throne surrounded by worship. The living creatures cried, Holy, holy, holy, LORD God Almighty, and the elders worshiped Him because He created all things. In God's hand was a scroll sealed with seven seals, and no one was found worthy to open it. Then John was told that the Lion of the tribe of Juda had prevailed, but when he looked he saw a Lamb as it had been slain. The Lamb is Jesus, who died for sinners. All heaven sang that the Lamb is worthy to receive power, riches, wisdom, strength, honour, glory and blessing. In chapter 7 John saw a great multitude from every nation standing before the throne.\n\nKey words: throne, worthy, Lamb, redeemed, multitude.\n\nYour task: (1) Tell what John saw in heaven in Revelation 4. (2) Write Revelation 5:12 in your own words. (3) Explain why Jesus is called both the Lion and the Lamb.\n\nCourse area: Bible elective. Scripture is read from the King James Version.", "video": "", "presenter": "Dr. Alex L. Jenkins Sr.", "standards": "Bible elective", "script": "In the last lesson we saw the risen Christ and His seven letters to the churches. Now John is invited into heaven. Revelation 4 describes the throne room of God. John saw a throne with One sitting on it, a rainbow around the throne, and living creatures and elders around it. The whole room was filled with worship.\n\nRevelation 4:8 says, And the four beasts had each of them six wings about him; and they were full of eyes within: and they rest not day and night, saying, Holy, holy, holy, LORD God Almighty, which was, and is, and is to come. Then the elders fell down before God and said in verse 11, Thou art worthy, O Lord, to receive glory and honour and power: for thou hast created all things, and for thy pleasure they are and were created. In heaven, everything centers on the worship of God. Our worship on earth is a small picture of that.\n\nIn chapter 5 John saw a scroll in God's right hand, sealed with seven seals. An angel asked who was worthy to open it, and no one could. John wept. Then one of the elders spoke to him. Revelation 5:5 says, And one of the elders saith unto me, Weep not: behold, the Lion of the tribe of Juda, the Root of David, hath prevailed to open the book, and to loose the seven seals thereof. John expected to see a lion. But look at what he saw next. Verse 6 says, And I beheld, and, lo, in the midst of the throne and of the four beasts, and in the midst of the elders, stood a Lamb as it had been slain. Jesus is the Lion of Judah because He is a king and a conqueror. He is the Lamb because He won the victory by giving His life for us.\n\nHeaven then broke into a new song. Revelation 5:9 says, And they sung a new song, saying, Thou art worthy to take the book, and to open the seals thereof: for thou wast slain, and hast redeemed us to God by thy blood out of every kindred, and tongue, and people, and nation; The redeemed come from every nation. And verse 12 says, Saying with a loud voice, Worthy is the Lamb that was slain to receive power, and riches, and wisdom, and strength, and honour, and glory, and blessing. Verse 13 shows that all creation joins in. Blessing, and honour, and glory, and power, be unto him that sitteth upon the throne, and unto the Lamb for ever and ever.\n\nIn chapter 7 John saw a picture of God's people. Revelation 7:9 says, After this I beheld, and, lo, a great multitude, which no man could number, of all nations, and kindreds, and people, and tongues, stood before the throne, and before the Lamb, clothed with white robes, and palms in their hands; And they cried, as verse 10 says, Salvation to our God which sitteth upon the throne, and unto the Lamb. In the middle of the troubles of this world, the book of Revelation lets us see the throne. God is still reigning.\n\nYour task is to tell what John saw in heaven in Revelation 4, write Revelation 5:12 in your own words, and explain why Jesus is called both the Lion and the Lamb. Then take the four question check.\n\nThe Lamb is worthy, and heaven is full of worship. Excellence, Faith, Purpose. I will see you in the next lesson."}$j$::jsonb where not exists (select 1 from public.course_items where course_id = cid and kind = 'topic' and title = $j$6.2 The Throne in Heaven and the Lamb Who Was Slain$j$);
end
$seed$;
